---
const stats = [
  { n: '1,000+', l: 'Instruments a day' },
  { n: '40%', l: 'Less spent on materials' },
  { n: '15 min', l: 'Urgent turnaround' },
  { n: '$1,200', l: 'Raised at my murder mystery' },
];
---

<section id="about" class="sec" aria-labelledby="about-h">
  <div class="wrap sec-grid">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="label"><span class="num">01</span>About</span></div>
    <div class="sec-body reveal">
      <h2 id="about-h">About me</h2>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I&rsquo;ve worked at Penn Dental Medicine since May 2023. I help over 200 dental students, 30 residents, and
          30 faculty every day across morning and afternoon clinics. The job is mostly getting the right instruments
          and implant parts to the right chair on time. It also takes careful attention to money and records: orders,
          vendor payments, and repair claims with Straumann, Nobel Biocare, Astra Tech, and 3i Biomet.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of what I&rsquo;ve improved there started with something that kept going wrong. Urgent requests needed to
          come back fast, so I built a pre-staging system around the sterilizer cycles. Closing looked different
          depending on who closed, so I wrote a checklist. And the inventory process that cut our materials spending
          by 40% was my idea. I developed it and put it in place.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before Penn I ran a delivery warehouse for The Rounds, usually as the only person on site. Outside of work I
          plan events for my parish, sing in a choir, and have been making music for over eleven years. I&rsquo;m
          finishing my bachelor&rsquo;s at Penn while working full time.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="stats">
        {stats.map((s) => (
          <div class="stat"><dt class="label muted">{s.l}</dt><dd><b>{s.n}</b></dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <p class="label">
        <span class="num">&#9670;</span>
        Outstanding Service Award from the Penn Dental Medicine class of 2024. Recognized again at the prosthodontics
        graduations in 2025 and 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
